Z||000000||Brian84SE||06-04-2000||06:46 AM||||I read instructions everywhere that said to remove the alternator from the bottom (jack the car up, remove alt. bracket, etc.) but I removed mine from the top by removing the dog bone and it's bracket. It sure seemed alot easier to do....<P>Did I come upon some great discovery or is this common knowledge?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||87ECO5||06-05-2000||08:44 AM||||When I replaced mine about five months ago, I brought it out the top too.  However, that was before I replaced the torque reaction strut and the motor mounts.  Now, the engine sits about 1.5 inches closer to the trunk wall.  I doubt the alternator would come out the top.<P>What condition are your mounts and TRS?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Brian84SE||06-05-2000||10:47 AM||||dog bone is brand new. Mounts in good shape.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||06-05-2000||09:00 PM||||took mine off the top<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||William Federle||06-05-2000||10:34 PM||||I took mine out the bottom but if I ever have to do it again, I'm going try to take it out the top. Did you have to remove anything else? I looked at taking it out the top but it seemed that the stuff that holds the throttle cable and cruise cable might be in the way.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||06-06-2000||02:26 AM||||From my memory, i had to remove the air cleaner, and the vacuum hoses.  I dont have cruise control, just one cable for the throttle.  I took off the strut mount and also the alternator mount that has three bolts holding it. ||||reg||1
